hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-67 : List (List Word)
hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-67 = cons "**Unstructured data as observation trace; frequency, measure, and order without probability.** Raw byte arrays, natural-language prose, genomes, sensory streams, and execution logs appear unstructured only relative to an absent external schema. Under Part I §1, every finite record h is an observation trace of an interacting process with finite local information. By `fiberEquiv` (Part I eq. 1.2), any structural observation o : H → O factors H losslessly into H ≃ Σ_{y:O} Fib_o(y). Encoding the residual fiber requires no probability concept and no subjective prior: only empirical frequency (multiplicity, `Bag`), discrete positive measure (cumulative integer counts partitioning a finite range), and order (`Enumeration`, Part I eq. 2.2, A ≃ Fin(n)). For 0th-order multiplicities m₁, …, mᵣ summing to n, Part I §4.4 (`lib/sort.cub`) already establishes the exact fiber cardinality n! / ∏ m_j!, whose rank in Fin(n! / ∏ m_j!) achieves empirical entropy purely by counting and order. Where a law descends through o (the condition of `no-observed-law`, Part I §1), the continuation fiber is contractible and costs 0 bits; where descent fails, the exact bit cost is L(h; o) = L_O(o(h)) + ⌈log₂ |Fib_o(o(h))|⌉ (or ⌈log₂(M / μ_y(h))⌉ under a discrete ordered measure μ_y of volume M on Fib_o(y))." (cons "" nil)
